e5-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">5.4</span> Decision Trees</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module5-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">5.5</span> Clustering</a></li></ul><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 6: Model Training</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module6-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">6.1</span> Train/Test Splits</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module6-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">6.2</span> Classification Metrics</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module6-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">6.3</span> Regression Metrics</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module6-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">6.4</span> Hyperparameter Tuning</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module6-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">6.5</span> Preventing Overfitting</a></li></ul><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 7: Deep Learning</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module7-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">7.1</span> Neural Networks</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module7-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">7.2</span> TensorFlow/Keras</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module7-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">7.3</span> CNNs</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module7-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">7.4</span> RNNs &amp; LSTMs</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module7-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">7.5</span> Transfer Learning</a></li></ul><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 8: Prompt Engineering</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module8-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">8.1</span> Intro to Prompting</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module8-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">8.2</span> Zero/Few-Shot &amp; CoT</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module8-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">8.3</span> System Prompts</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module8-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">8.4</span> Output Formatting</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module8-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">8.5</span> Advanced Patterns</a></li></ul><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 9: LLM Fundamentals</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">9.1</span> How LLMs Work</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">9.2</span> Major LLM Families</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">9.3</span> Tokens &amp; Context</a></li><li class="active"><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">9.4</span> Fine-Tuning vs ICL</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">9.5</span> Safety &amp; Limitations</a></li></ul><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 10: RAG Pipelines</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">10.1</span> What is RAG?</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">10.2</span> Document Chunking</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">10.3</span> Embeddings &amp; Vectors</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">10.4</span> Building RAG</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">10.5</span> RAG Evaluation</a></li></ul></aside><button class="sidebar-toggle" aria-label="Toggle sidebar">☰</button><main class="main"><div class="container"><div class="hero"><h1>Fine-Tuning vs In-Context Learning</h1><p class="hero-subtitle">Understand when to fine-tune models versus when in-context learning through prompting is sufficient. Master Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), LoRA, QLoRA, and compare them against few-shot and zero-shot learning approaches. Build a decision framework for your specific use case.</p></div><div class="objectives"><h3>Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Understand in-context learning: zero-shot, few-shot, and chain-of-thought prompting fundamentals</li><li>Learn Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) and when it provides value over prompt engineering</li><li>Master LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) for efficient fine-tuning with minimal computational resources</li><li>Understand QLoRA for ultra-efficient fine-tuning on consumer hardware</li><li>Build decision frameworks for choosing between in-context learning and fine-tuning</li><li>Implement and evaluate fine-tuning approaches with practical code examples</li><li>Understand trade-offs: latency, cost, capability, customization, and knowledge currency</li><li>Design evaluation strategies for comparing prompt-based vs fine-tuned approaches</li></ul></div><div class="callout info"><strong>Critical Framework:</strong> Modern LLM development has inverted traditional ML wisdom. Previously, fine-tuning was standard; in-context learning was aspirational. Today, in-context learning through careful prompting is often superior, faster, and cheaper than fine-tuning. Fine-tuning is now specialized, used when in-context learning reaches capability limits or cost constraints become binding.</div><section class="lesson-section"><h2>1. In-Context Learning: Prompting as Learning</h2><p>In-context learning is the surprising discovery that LLMs adapt to new tasks through examples in the prompt, without any training. GPT-3's key innovation was demonstrating this at scale: giving the model 2-5 examples of a task in the prompt enables it to solve new instances of that task, despite having never been trained on it. This is not prompt engineering in the sense of "finding clever wording"—it's genuine learning of task structure from context.</p><h4>Zero-Shot Learning</h4><p>Zero-shot: providing only task description, no examples. "Classify this email as spam or not: [email]". Success depends on model capability and how clearly you describe the task. Works well for tasks models likely encountered during training (common classification, summarization, Q&A). Fails for novel tasks or tasks requiring specific domain knowledge.</p><h4>Few-Shot Learning</h4><p>Few-shot: providing 2-5 examples of input-output pairs before the test case. The examples teach task structure. "Here are emails and classifications: [email1] spam [email2] not spam... now classify: [new email]". Few-shot outperforms zero-shot on most tasks, often dramatically. The key insight: you're not showing exhaustive examples; you're showing sufficient diversity of the task structure.</p><h4>Chain-of-Thought (CoT)</h4><p>Chain-of-Thought: asking the model to show reasoning steps before final answer. "Solve this step-by-step: [math problem]". Critical for complex reasoning. Models generate intermediate steps, reducing error rates significantly. "Let's think step by step" often improves performance 10-50% on reasoning tasks.</p><div class="code-block"><pre><span class="cm"># In-context learning examples</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="nm">openai</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">OpenAI</span>

<span class="va">client</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">OpenAI</span>()

<span class="cm"># Zero-shot: just description</span>
<span class="va">response</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">client</span>.<span class="fn">chat</span>.<span class="fn">completions</span>.<span class="fn">create</span>(
    <span class="va">model</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"gpt-4"</span>,
    <span class="va">messages</span><span class="op">=</span>[{
        <span class="st">"role"</span>: <span class="st">"user"</span>,
        <span class="st">"content"</span>: <span class="st">"Classify as positive, negative, or neutral: 'This product is amazing!'"</span>
    }]
)

<span class="cm"># Few-shot: with examples</span>
<span class="va">response</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">client</span>.<span class="fn">chat</span>.<span class="fn">completions</span>.<span class="fn">create</span>(
    <span class="va">model</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"gpt-4"</span>,
    <span class="va">messages</span><span class="op">=</span>[{
        <span class="st">"role"</span>: <span class="st">"user"</span>,
        <span class="st">"content"</span>: <span class="st">"""Classify sentiment:

"Great product, highly recommend" → Positive
"Broke after one week" → Negative
"It's a product" → Neutral

"This product is amazing!" → ?"""</span>
    }]
)

<span class="cm"># Chain-of-Thought: explicit reasoning</span>
<span class="va">response</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">client</span>.<span class="fn">chat</span>.<span class="fn">completions</span>.<span class="fn">create</span>(
    <span class="va">model</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"gpt-4"</span>,
    <span class="va">messages</span><span class="op">=</span>[{
        <span class="st">"role"</span>: <span class="st">"user"</span>,
        <span class="st">"content"</span>: <span class="st">"If a store sells apples at $3/lb and oranges at $2/lb, and I buy 5lbs apples and 3lbs oranges, how much did I spend? Show your work."</span>
    }]
)</pre></div></section><section class="lesson-section"><h2>2. When In-Context Learning Reaches Its Limits</h2><p>In-context learning succeeds for many tasks but has fundamental constraints. First: context window limits. Providing 100 examples requires 10K-20K tokens; this compresses your actual task context. Second: task complexity. Some tasks need more training signal than few examples provide. Third: consistency. Few-shot examples might show inconsistent behavior on edge cases. Fourth: cost. Each query includes full examples; costs scale with example count. When in-context learning plateaus—you've tried 5-shot, 10-shot, chain-of-thought, still not good enough—fine-tuning becomes valuable.</p></section><section class="lesson-section"><h2>3. Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT)</h2><p>Supervised Fine-Tuning updates model weights on your specific task data. GPT-3.5 started with base pretraining, then SFT on instruction-following examples, then RLHF. You can do similar for specific domains. SFT requires: training data (50-500 examples minimum, 1000+ for strong performance), infrastructure (GPUs), patience (hours-days of training), and evaluation discipline.</p><h4>When SFT Provides Value</h4><p>SFT is valuable when: (1) in-context learning reaches plateau despite optimization, (2) you have 500+ quality examples, (3) inference cost from full examples (in-context) becomes binding, (4) you want deterministic behavior on edge cases, (5) knowledge updates are frequent (new examples accumulate regularly).</p><h4>SFT Trade-offs</h4><p>Advantages: improved performance on specific domain, reduced example tokens needed (inference is cheaper), deterministic behavior, ability to encode domain knowledge. Disadvantages: expensive to train, slower iteration, domain-specific (doesn't generalize outside domain), requires maintaining dataset pipeline.</p></section><section class="lesson-section"><h2>4. LoRA and QLoRA: Efficient Fine-Tuning</h2><p>Full fine-tuning is expensive. GPT-4 has ~1.7 trillion parameters; fine-tuning all of them requires massive memory and compute. LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) is a breakthrough enabling efficient fine-tuning. Insight: most of the model is task-independent. We can freeze base weights and add small "adapter" layers that capture task-specific knowledge. These adapters are tiny (0.1% of model size) but sufficient for adaptation.</p><h4>LoRA Technical Approach</h4><p>Instead of updating W ∈ R^(d×d), introduce low-rank decomposition: ΔW = AB where A ∈ R^(d×r), B ∈ R^(r×d), r << d (typically r=8-64). Train A and B only; keep base W frozen. Huge memory/compute savings: adapters are 0.1% of base model size but capture sufficient task adaptation. Inference adds only minimal computation (matrix multiplications with small matrices).</p><h4>QLoRA: Ultra-Efficient Fine-Tuning</h4><p>QLoRA quantizes the base model to 4-bit, then applies LoRA. Enables fine-tuning 70B models on single GPU (previously required 8 GPUs). Training time: hours not weeks. This democratized fine-tuning—researchers without massive resources can now fine-tune frontier models.</p><div class="code-block"><pre><span class="cm"># LoRA fine-tuning with unsloth (efficient LoRA library)</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="nm">unsloth</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">FastLanguageModel</span>

<span class="cm"># Load 4-bit quantized Llama</span>
<span class="va">model</span>, <span class="va">tokenizer</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">FastLanguageModel</span>.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(
    <span class="va">model_name</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"unsloth/llama-3-8b-bnb-4bit"</span>,
    <span class="va">max_seq_length</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">2048</span>,
    <span class="va">dtype</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">None</span>,
    <span class="va">load_in_4bit</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">True</span>,
)

<span class="cm"># Add LoRA adapters</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">FastLanguageModel</span>.<span class="fn">get_peft_model</span>(
    <span class="va">model</span>,
    <span class="va">r</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">16</span>,  <span class="cm"># LoRA rank</span>
    <span class="va">lora_alpha</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">16</span>,
    <span class="va">lora_dropout</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.05</span>,
    <span class="va">bias</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"none"</span>,
    <span class="va">use_gradient_checkpointing</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"unsloth"</span>,
    <span class="va">use_rslora</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">True</span>,
)

<span class="cm"># Prepare training data</span>
<span class="va">training_data</span> <span class="op">=</span> [
    {<span class="st">"text"</span>: <span class="st">"<|begin_of_text|>User: [instruction]<|end_header_id|>Assistant: [expected response]"</span>},
    <span class="cm"># ... more examples</span>
]

<span class="cm"># Train (uses HuggingFace Trainer)</span>
<span class="cm"># Training 500 examples on single A100: 1-2 hours</span>
<span class="cm"># Training 5000 examples on single GPU: 10-20 hours</span></pre></div></section><section class="lesson-section"><h2>5. Decision Framework: ICL vs Fine-Tuning</h2><svg viewBox="0 0 900 500" class="lesson-svg"><rect width="900" height="500" fill="#f9f9f9"/><text x="450" y="30" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Decision Framework: In-Context Learning vs Fine-Tuning</text><rect x="50" y="70" width="350" height="400" fill="#e8f4f8" stroke="#4a90e2" stroke-width="2" rx="5"/><text x="225" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" font-size="14">In-Context Learning (ICL)</text><text x="75" y="125" font-size="12" font-weight="500">When to Choose:</text><text x="75" y="145" font-size="11">• Few examples suffice (&lt;5)</text><text x="75" y="160" font-size="11">• Task is standard/common</text><text x="75" y="175" font-size="11">• Quick iteration needed</text><text x="75" y="190" font-size="11">• Limited training data</text><text x="75" y="205" font-size="11">• Cost per query low</text><text x="75" y="230" font-size="12" font-weight="500">Advantages:</text><text x="75" y="250" font-size="11">• Zero training overhead</text><text x="75" y="265" font-size="11">• Fast iteration/experimentation</text><text x="75" y="280" font-size="11">• Broad generalization</text><text x="75" y="295" font-size="11">• Update examples instantly</text><text x="75" y="320" font-size="12" font-weight="500">Disadvantages:</text><text x="75" y="340" font-size="11">• Context window limits</text><text x="75" y="355" font-size="11">• Expensive at scale (examples in every query)</text><text x="75" y="370" font-size="11">• Inconsistent edge case behavior</text><rect x="500" y="70" width="350" height="400" fill="#f0e6f8" stroke="#722ed1" stroke-width="2" rx="5"/><text x="675" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" font-size="14">Fine-Tuning</text><text x="525" y="125" font-size="12" font-weight="500">When to Choose:</text><text x="525" y="145" font-size="11">• ICL plateaus (poor performance)</text><text x="525" y="160" font-size="11">• 500+ quality examples available</text><text x="525" y="175" font-size="11">• Inference cost is binding</text><text x="525" y="190" font-size="11">• Domain-specific terminology</text><text x="525" y="205" font-size="11">• Deterministic behavior needed</text><text x="525" y="230" font-size="12" font-weight="500">Advantages:</text><text x="525" y="250" font-size="11">• Better performance (usually)</text><text x="525" y="265" font-size="11">• Cheaper inference (no examples)</text><text x="525" y="280" font-size="11">• Deterministic behavior</text><text x="525" y="295" font-size="11">• Encodes domain knowledge</text><text x="525" y="320" font-size="12" font-weight="500">Disadvantages:</text><text x="525" y="340" font-size="11">• Training cost (compute time)</text><text x="525" y="355" font-size="11">• Domain-specific (limited generalization)</text><text x="525" y="370" font-size="11">• Slower iteration cycle</text></svg><p>Simple decision rule: try in-context learning first. Use zero-shot and few-shot prompting, optimize examples, try chain-of-thought. Only when ICL performance plateau is unacceptable and you have 500+ examples, consider fine-tuning. Default to cheapest approach (ICL with frontier model) unless specific constraints demand fine-tuning.</p></section><section class="lesson-section"><h2>6. Evaluation: Measuring Success</h2><p>Comparing approaches requires systematic evaluation. Test on held-out examples (don't evaluate on examples shown in prompts—that's not learning). Metrics depend on task: classification needs precision/recall; generation needs BLEU/ROUGE or human eval; reasoning needs correctness rate. Practical approach: evaluate same 100 test examples across (1) zero-shot baseline, (2) few-shot with 5 examples, (3) fine-tuned model. Compare cost/performance trade-off.</p></section><section class="lesson-section"><h2>7. Hybrid Approaches</h2><p>Modern practice combines both. Use fine-tuned models for baseline, then add in-context examples for specific edge cases. Or use retrieval to fetch similar examples from a database, then include them in prompt (RAG + few-shot). This hybrid approach often outperforms pure ICL or pure fine-tuning.</p></section><section class="lesson-section"><h2>8. Key Takeaways</h2><ul><li><strong>In-context learning is surprisingly powerful:</strong> Modern LLMs learn from examples in prompts. Try it first before fine-tuning.</li><li><strong>Few-shot is simple and effective:</strong> 2-5 examples often dramatically improve performance. Chain-of-thought thinking further improves reasoning.</li><li><strong>Fine-tuning has high upfront cost:</strong> Worth only when ICL plateaus and you have sufficient data.</li><li><strong>LoRA enables accessible fine-tuning:</strong> Fine-tune 70B models on single GPU. Dramatically lowered barrier to entry.</li><li><strong>Evaluate systematically:</strong> Don't optimize on examples shown in prompt. Test on held-out data.</li><li><strong>Hybrid approaches are practical:</strong> Combine ICL and fine-tuning for best results in production.</li><li><strong>Cost-performance trade-off matters:</strong> In-context learning with frontier model often beats fine-tuned smaller model on cost/performance.</li></ul></section><div class="lesson-exercise"><h3>Practice Exercise</h3><p><strong>Design an approach for a classification task:</strong></p><ol><li>Start with zero-shot baseline: test on 100 examples, measure accuracy</li><li>Add few-shot examples (5 examples): measure improvement</li><li>Try chain-of-thought: ask model to explain reasoning first</li><li>If performance insufficient, design fine-tuning approach: estimate data needed, compute cost, training time</li><li>Compare: ICL inference cost vs fine-tuned inference cost at scale (1M predictions/year)</li><li>Recommend approach with cost/performance justification</li></ol></div><nav class="lesson-nav"><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson3" class="nav-button prev">Previous Lesson</a><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson5" class="nav-button next">Next Lesson</a></nav></div></main><script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@supabase/supabase-js@2/dist/umd/supabase.min.js"></script>
